Taking your teams out of the office and settling them somewhere quiet, in nature, a few hours from Paris: that is the idea behind a corporate retreat in the countryside. A private estate, meeting rooms, everyone staying on site, and the work moving forward differently. At the Château de la Huberdière, in Touraine near Amboise, your company has fourteen hectares reserved for it alone, 2 hrs 20 from Paris.

I host these groups all year round, and the same questions come up before booking: what a countryside retreat really is, why it works, how far to look, and how to choose the right venue. Here is what you need to decide.

## What is a corporate retreat in the countryside

A countryside retreat gathers a team in a setting surrounded by nature, out in the country rather than in a city centre or a business hotel. The principle is simple: you leave the usual work backdrop behind to focus and reconnect.

In practice, that means a country estate, often privately hired, where the group works during the day and stays on site in the evening. No corridors shared with other companies, no city noise, no temptation to head home between sessions. For the length of the seminar, the place becomes the team's headquarters. It is this residential format that sets a countryside retreat apart from a simple away day.

## Why the countryside changes a corporate seminar

The countryside changes a corporate seminar because the setting acts on the way teams work and talk to each other. Taking staff out of their office habits creates a break, and that break helps them think differently.

Three effects come back with every seminar I host. First, disconnection: away from open-plan offices and notifications, attention refocuses on the day's subject. Then cohesion, because everything is shared, meals, breaks, evenings, and not only the meeting room. Finally wellbeing, now a genuine CSR criterion: a walk through the grounds and a clean break leave teams in better shape than a run of meetings in a basement.

The Huberdière is not a standardised seminar centre. It is a lived-in sixteenth-century family home, where I welcome your teams as guests. I do not run one group after another: for the whole length of your seminar, the château is yours, nobody else's.

## A corporate retreat 2 hrs 20 from Paris

The right distance for a seminar near Paris is around two hours: enough for a change of scene, little enough not to sacrifice a day to travel. The château sits precisely in that window, 2 hrs 20 by road.

For teams travelling by train, allow one hour by TGV to Saint-Pierre-des-Corps station, in Tours, then thirty minutes by car. Tours is 30 minutes from the estate. That accessibility changes everything for logistics: you leave in the morning, open the seminar at midday, head back the next evening, and the journey never eats into the programme. That is what makes a countryside retreat in the Loire Valley easier to organise than a far-off destination, with a real change of air in the bargain.

## What you need to work in the countryside

To work in the countryside, you first need real workspaces, not just a fine setting. The estate offers several configurations depending on the size of the group and the shape of the day.

- The Orangery, 200 sq m where light pours through the tall windows, for plenary sessions and large-group workshops
- Four more intimate lounges for breakout groups, one-to-ones or quiet time
- The grounds and terrace for open-air sessions, when the weather allows

The technical logistics follow: wifi, sound system and screens are provided. You arrive with your programme, and I set up the rooms in a U-shape, in islands or in a circle to suit your needs. The full details of the spaces, capacities and packages are on the dedicated page to [organise your seminar at the château](/en/chateau-seminar-loire).

For accommodation, the château sleeps up to 21 people in 10 rooms, each with a private bathroom. Staying residentially extends the conversations over dinner and builds cohesion beyond the meetings. The cooking is homemade: Lodovica, Italian by birth, prepares meals from market and kitchen-garden produce, and caters to everyone's dietary needs. For a group dinner or a special evening, discover [the château's table](/en/chateau-dining-touraine) too. Packages start at 70 € excl. VAT per person, and I draw up a precise quote based on the length, the number of participants and the activities.

## Residential or away day: which to choose

Between residential and away day, the real question is not only where to hold your seminar, but how long to stay. A residential seminar, over one or several days with a night on site, builds a cohesion a single day never reaches.

On an away day, the group works well, then scatters in the evening: everyone goes home with their tiredness and their own concerns, and the thread breaks. Residentially, the evening is part of the seminar. It is over dinner, on a walk through the grounds or during an impromptu card game that people open up and the real conversations happen. Managers often tell me as much: it is these off-programme moments that stay with people, long after they are back at the office.

For a year kick-off, a merger of teams or a project that calls for rebuilding trust, residential is the way. For a short, operational check-in, a day will do. I host both formats with the same standard of welcome, and I help you frame the right length for your objective.

## Choosing your countryside retreat well

Choosing your countryside retreat well comes down to a few concrete criteria, beyond the château photo. Before booking, ask these questions.

- Private hire: is the estate reserved for your company alone, or shared with other groups? At the Huberdière, exclusive use is total, fourteen hectares for you alone.
- Residential: can the whole group stay on site? That is what separates an away day from a genuine cohesion seminar.
- Distance: a venue 2 hrs 20 from Paris stays reachable in a morning, by car or by train.
- Catering: homemade cooking, able to adapt to dietary needs, beats an imposed caterer.
- Activities: does the venue offer real opportunities to bring the team together?

On that last point, Touraine is well supplied, and I book the activities with our local partners: an Italian cooking class with Lodovica, a hot-air balloon flight at dawn, a canoe trip down the Cher, a tasting in the Vouvray cellars, or a visit to Amboise and the Clos Lucé. These informal moments create shared memories and change how colleagues see one another. To go further, my guide to [visiting the Loire châteaux](/en/blog/visiting-loire-chateaux) will give your teams ideas, and the full article on [organising a seminar at the château](/en/blog/chateau-seminar-guide) details how a typical day unfolds.

Tell me your dates, the size of the group and the spirit of the seminar: I will send you a tailored proposal within 24 hours.
